# CORE #11/#12 REGRESSION — `for s in d:` over a struct-payload `Deque[S]`.
#
# AXIS PIN: `for`-loop iterable · Deque[S] struct payload · elem-type
# `Deque__S` → resolved via `infer_collection_element_type` (methods.rs).
#
# RED-VERIFIED PRE-FIX (Round XXIII γ scout 2026-08-01): printed
# `0 / 0` on BOTH backends — `infer_collection_element_type` had no
# `Deque__` arm, so the element type fell through to `I64_TYPE`, the
# struct-field walk found nothing, and the loop body read zeros.
# `Deque[int]` iteration was accidentally correct (Core #15e Q6:
# green for a reason unrelated to what it tests) because the
# `I64_TYPE` fallback matches `int`.
#
# INTENDED post-fix: `10 / 20`, matching the Vector twin.
from std.collections import Deque

struct S:
    int fd

void main():
    Deque[S] d = Deque[S]()
    d.push(S(10))
    d.push(S(20))
    for s in d:
        print(s.fd)
